          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly presents Cordelia's love for Lear as true. Its primary force is a restrained avowal, regardless of whether its sincerity can be independently established.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"The focal avowal is syntactically qualified by an invocation of Cordelia's existing filial bond. She presents her love as fulfilling that bond rather than making a new promise. Her following explanation identifies the associated duties as obedience, love, and honour.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Obey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression is a controlled answer explaining the extent and basis of Cordelia's love, not a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.89,\"reason\":\"The focal expression's core force is adequately represented by avowal and its invocation of filial obligation. Its restrained contrast with her sisters' claims does not require a separate residual mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say who loves him most, linking their answers to the distribution of his kingdom. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia gives this measured avowal, limiting it by her filial bond rather than adopting her sisters' expansive rhetoric.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes an existing filial bond rather than undertaking a new future commitment. She describes that bond as requiring her to return fitting duties by obeying, loving, and honouring Lear. Lear does not accept the answer as adequate; he tells her to amend it lest she damage her fortunes. The supplied passage does not show final uptake beyond that immediate rejection of its sufficiency.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare's King Lear. The classification relies only on the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports that Cordelia represents herself as genuinely loving Lear, especially through her asides, but literary dialogue cannot conclusively establish inner sincerity. The focal phrase itself does not specify the detailed terms of the filial bond; those are clarified by the surrounding speech.\"}"
